 *Resource Allocation:* The prompt states that approval means committing implementation resources. Is this *too* theoretical? The proposal mentions validation on existing lab deployments, which grounds it in practice.\n\n    *   The core of Art 12(2) is determining \"relevance.\" A formal framework to define relevance removes ambiguity and creates a standardized, defensible process for the R&D institution across all its high-risk systems.\n    *   It transforms a liability (logging overhead/legal uncertainty) into an asset (better observability).\n    *   From a compliance perspective: This is highly valuable because it provides a methodology to justify *why* certain events are logged and others aren't, which is exactly what a regulator will ask during an audit.\n\n    *   Rationale: Formalizing \"relevance\" under Article 12(2) transforms a vague legal requirement into a defensible technical standard, significantly reducing the risk of regulatory non-compliance and enhancing system safety.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (around 85-90). It's not 100 because it is a \"research programme\" which carries some execution risk, but the potential payoff for compliance and reputation is huge.",
