rces on a *public audit tool* help me?\n    *   Pros: It forces internal standardization of logging schemas (making the core product better/more viable). It removes friction for the end-user/deployer.\n    *   Cons: It diverts 3 engineers away from actual AI development or core infrastructure toward \"compliance tooling.\" It's more of a marketing/legal play (\"market differentiator,\" \"building trust\").\n\n    *   The EU AI Act requires that high-risk systems *technically allow* for recording.\n    *   If the lab builds these systems, they need to implement the logging *inside* the system first. This proposal is about a tool to *verify* those logs later.\n    *   From a Dev Engineer's perspective: If we haven't standardized how our high-risk systems log events, building an audit toolkit will be a nightmare because every project will have different formats. Conversely, if we build the toolkit first (or in parallel), it provides a target specification for all internal dev teams to follow. This actually *improves* prototyping quality by providing a clear \"definition of done\" for the logging requirement.\n\n    *   Rationale: Building a standardized audit tool forces an internal unified logging schema, which reduces technical debt and improves the consistency of our high-risk AI prototypes. The scope (3 people, 2 quarters) is realistic and technically straightforward.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High, but not 100 because it's slightly more focused on marketing/compliance than core R&D, but the engineering benefits of standardization are strong. Let's say 85.\n\n    *   Rationale: (Focus on technical viability/prototyping). \"Establishing a standardized, open-source verification tool incentivizes internal consistency in logging schemas across different AI projects, reducing fragmented implementation and ensuring high-quality, predictable output for regulatory audits.\"\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85.",
