uable.\n\n    *   *Positive:* Automating logs ensures that experimental runs are traceable. It prevents the \"black box\" problem where we don't remember exactly what version of data or which specific call led to a result. This supports the intellectual merit of research by ensuring rigorous record-keeping of experiments.\n    *   *Negative:* It doesn't *create* new knowledge; it just implements a regulatory requirement efficiently.\n\n    *   The goal is to ensure high-risk AI systems (which these prototypes will become) are compliant without slowing down the actual R&D process.\n    *   From my perspective as a researcher, any tool that removes administrative/compliance overhead while maintaining data integrity for later analysis is a win. It allows us to focus on the *frontier* rather than the *boilerplate*.\n\n    *   Rationale: Automating the capture of inference and data events ensures rigorous traceability and reproducibility without impeding the speed of iterative experimentation. This reduces technical debt and prevents the loss of critical experimental metadata necessary for scientific auditing.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: High (e.g., 85-90). It's a practical necessity that protects the integrity of the research output.",
