        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2601\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This module establishes the data provenance and traceability necessary for the rigorous scientific auditing and error analysis of biometric systems. While primarily a compliance measure, it provides the empirical infrastructure required to validate system performance and investigate failure modes in real-world applications.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 70\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nKnowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, broader scientific impact.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71. Focus on traceability, logs for high-risk AI, and specific requirements for biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)).\n\n        *   Rationale (1-2 sentences).\n        *   Decision (\"approve\" or \"reject\").\n        *   Promise (0-100 integer).\nScreen proposals independently from my professional vantage point. No quota, no ranking. Approve if it clears the bar.\n\n    *   ID: P2601\n    *   Text: \"Reference-Database Provenance Tracker for Biometric Systems.\" It's a data-governance module that records reference database consultations, input data for positive hits, and identities of verifiers (per Art 14(5)). Uses an immutable audit ledger. Specifically targets the requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\n\n    *   *Perspective:* As a Researcher concerned with the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and scientific impact...\n    *   *Evaluation:* This proposal is primarily an engineering/compliance task (implementing logging/audit trails). It doesn't seem to push the boundaries of AI research or introduce new scientific methodologies. However, from a \"scientific impact\" perspective, ensuring provenance and traceability in high-risk systems is fundamental for reproducibility and auditing in AI safety and ethics research.\n    *   *Wait:* Does it have intellectual merit? Not really. It's a software implementation to meet a legal requirement.\n    *   *But,* as an R&D institution, we can't conduct valid research on high-risk systems if those systems are non-compliant or lack the traceability needed for scientific verification of their behavior in real-world settings. If our biometric systems aren't traceable, we can't scientifically analyze failure modes or bias across different