ns (where research must meet regulatory standards to be viable): This is essential infrastructure. It ensures that the \"scientific impact\" isn't negated by legal failures.\n    *   Crucially, Article 12 requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" A CI gate ensuring this capability exists before code merge is a direct implementation of the requirement.\n\n    *   Rationale: Ensuring that traceability and logging are built-in from the prototype stage preserves the scientific integrity of our experiments and ensures our research can actually be translated into deployable, compliant systems.\n    *   Decision: Approve. (It's an efficient way to manage a hard requirement without derailing researchers with manual paperwork later).\n    *   Promise: High. It's a standard software engineering best practice applied to regulatory compliance.\n\n    *   Wait, as a Researcher focusing on the *knowledge frontier*, do I care about CI pipelines?\n    *   Yes, because \"intellectual merit\" includes the reproducibility of results. If we cannot log events (as per Art 12), we cannot reproduce errors or successes in high-risk systems.\n    *   Furthermore, if the system is designed such that logging is an afterthought, it often leads to architectural debt that hinders future research into interpretability and auditing.\n\n    *   Rationale: Integrating automated log validation into the development cycle ensures scientific reproducibility and guarantees that our research outputs remain viable for real-world application under regulatory constraints.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85. (High because it's practical and reduces \"compliance debt,\" though not a breakthrough in AI science itself).",
