nts for EU AI Act compliance (which are high-level system events: start/end time, database refs, etc.) would affect gradient dynamics—is scientifically weak.\n    *   Article 12 refers to *event logs* (e.g., \"who used it,\" \"when did it start\"), not necessarily per-step tensor logging or telemetry that would interfere with the backward pass of a neural network. Even if it were detailed telemetry, the impact on generalisation is typically zero unless the logging induces some form of stochasticity or precision change (which is poor engineering).\n    *   The \"intellectual merit\" here seems forced. It's attempting to turn a bookkeeping requirement into a research project without a strong theoretical basis for why it would be interesting or impactful.\n\n    *   Rationale: The hypothesis that regulatory event logging impacts gradient dynamics and generalization lacks a sound theoretical basis, as such logging is typically decoupled from the model's computational graph. Restructuring the entire experimentation platform to investigate this unlikely behavioral delta is an inefficient use of research resources.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 10 (very low, because while observability *is* a field, \"compliance logs\" are not \"observability that changes the math\").",
