y, not necessarily a \"public benchmark\" to elevate \"intellectual rigour.\"\n        *   The goal is compliance. The most direct path to compliance is implementing the logging requirements (Article 12(3) specifics). A scientific study on \"log utility\" as a measurable quantity is high-effort research that doesn't directly translate into the technical viability of our specific AI systems. It slows down prototyping by shifting focus from \"build it\" to \"measure how we might build it.\"\n\n    *   The proposal is more academic/research-oriented than engineering-oriented.\n    *   As a Dev Engineer focused on speed and technical viability, I want tools that help me implement the law, not a scientific paper on log fidelity.\n    *   It doesn't solve the requirement of *having* the logs; it solves the problem of *measuring* them across different schemes. While useful for an R&D institution generally, from a \"prototyping and viability\" perspective, it's a distraction.\n\n    *   Rationale: This is a research-oriented benchmarking exercise that does not provide a concrete technical implementation or acceleration for building compliant logging systems. It prioritizes academic rigor over the immediate engineering need to deploy viable record-keeping mechanisms.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 30 (It has some value for long-term standardization, but low utility for current prototyping speed/viability).",
