demic.\n\n    *   The prompt says: \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources... not funding to explore.\"\n    *   This proposal asks for a working group, piloting, and publication. This is an investment of *human capital* (researchers).\n    *   Is it efficient? Mapping regulatory fields to ML reporting practices is a necessary first step before coding logs. Doing it as a standard reduces duplication across the lab's projects.\n\n    *   The focus on \"knowledge frontier\" and \"citable contribution\" appeals to scientific ambition but might be an inefficient way to handle a legal requirement. But, for a high-end R&D institution, establishing the standard *is* how they maintain leadership and attract funding/talent.\n    *   Compared to just telling every engineer \"figure out Article 12 on your own,\" this is far more efficient (centralized template + schema).\n\n    *   Rationale: Reframing compliance as a reproducibility standard provides a scalable way to implement logging across multiple projects, avoiding redundant effort while enhancing the lab's scientific prestige.\n    *   Decision: Approve. (It provides a structural solution—the schema and template—rather than just an ad-hoc one).\n    *   Promise: 75. It's high because it solves the \"how\" for multiple projects, but not 100 because the academic overhead (working group/publication) is a slight drain on pure implementation speed.\n\n    *Wait*, let me re-evaluate as a *strictly* resource-efficient Lab Director.\n    Is a white paper necessary? No.\n    Is a working group with three other labs efficient? That sounds like a nightmare of meetings and coordination (resource inefficient).\n    The goal is compliance. The \"citable contribution\" part is a bonus, not the core need.\n    If I just want the logs, I'd rather have an internal technical spec than a peer-reviewed paper.\n\n    *Counter-argument:* If we don't lead the standard, we follow someone else's (possibly inefficient) one. Leading the schema repository is a strategic move for infrastructure efficiency.\n\n    Let's stick with Approve, but keep the promise score moderate because of the administrative bloat associated with \"working groups\" and \"peer-reviewed publications.\"",
