cessary pillar is better than none.\n    *   *Market Reputation:* Aligning regulatory compliance with academic publishing standards increases the perceived quality of research and appeals to funders.\n\n    *   The proposal focuses on the *documentation* aspect mentioned in Recital 71 (\"keeping records and the availability of technical documentation\").\n    *   It does *not* address the \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" mandated by Article 12(1).\n    *   However, since it aims to systematize the \"clear and comprehensive form\" required for traceability and compliance verification, it is a highly valuable risk-mitigation strategy. It turns a regulatory burden into a competitive advantage (better publications/grants).\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal systematically addresses the technical documentation requirements of Recital 71 by integrating them into existing research workflows, thereby reducing compliance risk while enhancing institutional reputation with funders.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High. It's a low-friction way to ensure that the \"paper trail\" for high-risk AI systems is created consistently across projects.\n\n    *   Id: P2786.\n    *   Rationale: (As above).\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 90.",
